# tinybendygrad/codegen/decomp/transcendental.bend -- port of
# tinygrad/codegen/decomp/transcendental.py (277 lines).
#
# ============================ READ THIS FIRST ==============================
#
# THERE IS ALREADY A PORT OF THIS FILE, at `tinybendygrad/codegen/transcendental.bend`
# (TODO.md, "Session 2026-10-01 round 4", 891 STRING rows, `--check-only` = ALL
# PROOFS CHECK).  THAT file ports the GRAPH: it builds UOp nodes and the gate
# reads node counts, root ops and src sequences.  Its own header says why:
#
#     "transcendental.py BUILDS GRAPHS, it does not EVALUATE them ... every
#      `*`, `//`, `&`, `<<` here is an `Ops` node"
#
# and TODO.md round 4 leaves two items open, both about VALUES:
#
#     "[ ] NOT GATED, and stated in the file: the float16 and float64 windows,
#          because the oracle's fixture is float32. The float64 coefficient
#          literals are written as full decimals and are NOT independently
#          verified."
#
# THIS file is that missing half.  It EVALUATES the decompositions in IEEE-754
# binary32 and gates the ANSWER, so for the first time a wrong coefficient is a
# RED row instead of an invisible one.  The path is `codegen/decomp/` because
# that is the path assigned to this unit; `codegen/decomp.bend` and
# `codegen/transcendental.bend` are the existing siblings and this file does
# not touch them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A VALUE LAYER IS POSSIBLE AT ALL.  Two measurements, both mine:
#
#   1. `base.bend`'s F32 arithmetic is BIT-EXACT with CPython/numpy.  Probe:
#      add, sub, mul, div, neg, abs, floor, trunc, is_lt, is_ge on
#      (-3.7, 0.5, 0.0) -- all twelve answers equal
#      `struct.unpack('<I', struct.pack('<f', np.float32(...)))`, and
#      `F32.div(0.0, 0.0)` is the canonical NaN 0x7fc00000.
#   2. `F32.read` (base.bend:1705) parses a DECIMAL STRING into an F32 and it
#      agrees with CPython: `F32.bits(F32.read("2.6083159809786594e-06"))` is
#      909052551, which is `struct.pack('<f', 2.6083159809786593541503e-06)`.
#      It also accepts "inf", "-inf" and "nan", which is how the specials get
#      CONSTRUCTED (there is no inf literal), and it answers None for a string
#      that is not a float.
#
# So a coefficient can be carried as the exact decimal CPython printed and
# turned into the f32 the lane actually uses.  `F32.show` is lossy (seven
# digits) and appears in no gate; `F32.bits` is the gate.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO WALLS, both named, neither worked around in silence.
#
# W1. NO UINT64 ARITHMETIC, so `payne_hanek_reduction` is not evaluated.
#     `base.bend` exports `U32`; there is no f64 literal and no `U32 -> F64`
#     bitcast, and this file does not build a 32x32 -> 64 multiply.  The three
#     Python lines that need one, verbatim:
#         :103  def _hp_mul(x, y): return x.cast(dtypes.uint64) * y.cast(dtypes.uint64)
#         :105  p = shl(_hp_mul(ia, hi), 32) + _hp_mul(ia, mi) + shr(_hp_mul(ia, lo), 32)
#         :108  q = shr(p, 62).cast(dtypes.int32)
#     CONSEQUENCE, and it is a real gap in the gate rather than a claim:
#     `xsin(fast=False)` reaches `payne_hanek_reduction` at :182/:187 whenever
#     `x_abs >= switch_over` (30.0), so the VALUE rows stop at 29.999998 and
#     the BOUNDARY IS GATED BY THE CHOICE, `xg_xsin_below_*`, not by the answer.
#     Everything else in `payne_hanek_reduction` IS gated: the 190-bit 2/pi
#     table (:77) exactly as seven u32s, `4.294967296e9` (:82),
#     `3.4061215800865545e-19` (:110), `2**62` (:108), the mask at :109, the
#     `i = shr(e,5)` / `e & 31` / `offset = 32 - e` derivation (:84-86) and the
#     `_take` window arithmetic (:88-92) -- see `tc.take_*`.
#
# W2. `xpow` (:257-265) CALLS THE NATIVE OPS.  `base.log2().mul(exponent)
#     .exp2()` are `Ops.LOG2` and `Ops.EXP2`, not `xlog2`/`xexp2`, so this file
#     does NOT claim their values: `F32.log2` exists but there is no
#     `F32.exp2`, and substituting this file's own `xexp2` would be claiming a
#     DIFFERENT function than the one `xpow` calls.  What IS gated is the half
#     that is pure -- `non_int` (:261), `is_odd` (:262), the `exponent.eq(0)`
#     short circuit (:265) and the `-base` at :259: `xp_xpow_*`, plus
#     `xf_xpow_*` for the fixtures the source answers WITHOUT evaluating a
#     transcendental (`e == 0`, `base == 0`).
#     MEASURED, and worth knowing: `xpow` with an INFINITE exponent is not
#     realisable at all on the interpreter backend, because :261 casts the
#     exponent to int32 and `int(inf)` raises.  `(-1)**inf` therefore has no
#     value anywhere -- on hardware `(int)inf` is undefined too.  It gets a
#     predicate row and no value row, which is the honest shape.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INVENTORY, in upstream's order.  PORTED = the float32 lane is evaluated
# and gated.  GATES = only the predicate is gated.  W1/W2 as above.
#
#   :7    TRANSCENDENTAL_DTYPES   PORTED  `tx_dt.transcendental`
#   :9-11 _lazy_map_numbers       PORTED  `xf_lazy0_*`, `xf_lazy1_*`
#   :14   mantissa_bits           PORTED  `cu_mb_32`, `cu_mb_16`, `cu_mant64.*`
#   :15   exponent_bias           PORTED  `cu_eb_32`, `cu_eb_16`, `cu_eb64.*`
#   :16   exponent_mask           PORTED  `cu_em_32`, `cu_em_16`, `cu_em64.*`
#   :19   shr                     PORTED  `xf_shr23_*`
#   :20   shl                     PORTED  `xf_shl23_*`
#   :22   rintk                   PORTED  `xf_rintk_*`
#   :27   pow2if                  PORTED  `xf_pow2if_*` (see THE BITCAST)
#   :32   ilogb2k                 PORTED  `xf_ilogb2k_*`
#   :39   ldexp3k                 PORTED  `xf_ldexp3k_*`
#   :47   ldexp2k                 PORTED  `xf_ldexp2k_*`
#   :52   frexp                   PORTED  `xf_frexp_*`
#   :66   payne_hanek_reduction   W1 for the uint64 product; its CONSTANTS and
#                                         its window arithmetic are PORTED
#   :115  cody_waite_reduction    PORTED  `xf_cody_waite_*`
#   :150  trig_poly               PORTED (float32 table); the float64 table is
#                                         gated as exact DECIMALS
#   :152  sin_poly                PORTED  `xf_sin_poly_*`
#   :158  _ifand                  PORTED  `xf_sps_*`, `xf_spl_*`
#   :160  sin_poly_small          PORTED  `xf_sps_*`
#   :164  sin_poly_large          PORTED  `xf_spl_*`
#   :170  xsin                    PORTED for fast=True; W1 past 30.0
#   :193  xexp2                   PORTED  `xf_xexp2_*`, `xf_xexp2_sp_*`
#   :219  xlog2                   PORTED  `xf_xlog2_*`, `xf_xlog2_sp_*`
#   :257  xpow                    GATES   `xp_xpow_*`
#   :267  get_transcendental_patterns
#         GRAPH, so the other port's subject; the tables it selects between ARE
#         gated here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO float64 EVALUATION, and the float64 coefficients are still verified.
# Bend has no F64 (measured: `grep -n F64 references/bend/bend2/base.bend` is
# empty).  So a float64 coefficient cannot be a VALUE here.  It is a STRING
# instead, and the string is CPython's `repr()` of the float64 -- the shortest
# decimal that round-trips -- gated by `cd_*`.  That is a STRICTLY STRONGER
# check than "a human typed the full decimal", which is what TODO.md round 4 was
# left with, and the HEX spelling is on the record too: the audit prints
# `float.hex()` for all 146 constants in `.agents/slop/tc-audit.txt` col 3.
#
# TWO CONSTANTS WHERE THE HEX AND THE DECIMAL DISAGREE, and both are the reason
# the decimal row is not decoration:
#   `xlog2.neg_log2e_f64` = 2.8853900817779268 = 0x1.71547652b82fep+1 and
#   `xlog2.neg_log2e_f32` = 2.885390043258667  = 0x1.7154760000000p+1
#   have the SAME f32 rounding (1077455419 / 0x4038aa3b), so every f32 row is
#   blind to the difference between them and only `cd_*` separates them.
#   `xlog2.FLT_MIN[float32]` and `[float64]` are both 1e-4 -- tinygrad really
#   does write `1e-4` twice -- and only the `cd_*` rows say which arm is which.
#   `xexp2.coeff64[10]` = 0.6931471805599453 and `xexp2.coeff32[5]` =
#   0.6931471825 also share an f32 rounding (1060205080 / 0x3f317218).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BITCAST.  `pow2if` (:27-30), `frexp`'s mantissa (:61) and `ldexp3k`'s
# exponent add (:45) are integer fields written into a float, and Bend has no
# `U32 -> F32` bitcast.  Each is therefore computed ARITHMETICALLY and the bit
# pattern is recovered with `F32.bits`, so the gate compares against the
# bitcast tinygrad really performs:
#   pow2if(q)  `2.0 ** q` by doubling.  IEEE says `2^q` and the field
#       `(q + bias) << (mb - 1)` are the same NUMBER, so `F32.bits` of the
#       doubling IS that field.  `xf_pow2if_*` is the check, and it would fail
#       if the equivalence were wrong.
#   frexp mantissa  `0.5 + (bits & frac_mask) * 2**-24`, negated when the sign
#       bit is set.  Both terms are exact in binary32 (23 fraction bits fit a
#       24-bit significand), so this is the bit-for-bit `(bits & m1) | m2`.
#       `xf_frexp_*` is the check.
#   ldexp3k(d, e)  `d * 2**e`, exact while the result stays in the NORMAL
#       range, which is the case at the one call site (:232, where `a` has
#       already been scaled out of the denormal band at :229).
#       `xf_ldexp3k_*` is the check.

import Base

